as part of a well-deserved victory lap  director bong joon ho  created a black and white version of  [Music]  parasite  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  thanks to mubi for sponsoring this video  get your 30 day free trial at mubi.com  thomasflight in our modern era black and  white filmmaking kind of continues to  limp forward there's a handful of  releases  every year and occasionally a more  prominent release  recent examples include alfonso quran's  roma  the lighthouse from last year or mank  this year david fincher's new film  but even though it's rare these days  black and white is an important aspect  of filmmaking and film history to  examine some of the greatest films ever  made are in black and white  and even today it provides a different  visual palette for  filmmakers to work with so with this  parasite release it's an interesting  opportunity not just to see a modern  film in black and white but to be able  to compare  the color version against the black and  white version to see how it affects the  experience of watching the film  and what the strengths of each version  are it gave me a new perspective on  certain aspects of the story and i also  think the experience of watching it  suffers in a few ways i'll look at the  film in four sections how black and  white  emphasizes texture how it changes how we  perceive  light in the scene how it changes the  emotion and the context of  some scenes and finally the downsides  and how i think the experience suffers  from the shift to black and white  and talking about black and white  cinematography texture is often one of  the first things that comes up  and it's cliche only because it's true  shooting  in black and white really does emphasize  texture  it's not that you can't see the textures  in a color version of the film  but without color your eyes are drawn  to texture they tend to pop a lot more  on the screen i found that the textures  of the environments became a lot more  obvious the dirtiness and the complex  and cluttered  aspect of the poor family's house and  neighborhood  stood in much starker contrast to the  rich neighborhoods  cleanliness cleaner lines and more  simple environment  again when i point these things out you  can definitely see them in the color  version  but in watching the film the black and  white this contrast between the two  stood out more to me than it had in any  of the previous  times i watched the film and color  watching the color version i always  thought more about the differences in  amount and quality of light between the  two houses  and also the difference in openness of  space  but watching in black and white i think  the difference in cleanliness  precision of design and quality of  materials becomes more obvious  this emphasis on texture also made me  notice an aspect of the park family home  design that i hadn't noticed before the  textured concrete  walls that are used in some rooms the  way they have a sort of faux roughness  and the irony of that contrasted against  the actual  roughness of the concrete in the kim's  environment  became more apparent to me the emphasis  on texture has an interesting impact on  this scene where we descend  into the basement of the park home the  staircase in the black and white version  to me felt more like an  extension of the house instead of a  separate area the division between the  two spaces  feels less distinct and you can feel the  shift and texture  as things get dirtier and grimier during  the descent  into the basement some small details  stand out a bit more  for instance in this scene mr kemp's  face seems a little dirtier to me and  black and white  and i think the emphasis on the texture  of the scholar stone and the black and  white version  gives it a really interesting visual  quality that i don't think it has  in the color version in this shot the  harsh distinction between the sunlight  and the shadow  is more clear and i think it makes the  symbolism of mr kim crossing a line  a little bit more noticeable the  emphasis on texture adds some  interesting elements to the experience  of watching the film  but it's not necessarily a benefit  overall because you're also  losing any of the benefits you'd get  from the color in the film  which i'll talk about later perhaps the  most interesting and surprising thing to  me and the shift to black and white is  the way it affects how we perceive  light in the scene the contrast between  two different colors can help us  draw a distinction between two details  in a scene  even if they have the same tonal value  in black and white the distinction  between  every detail has to be defined by tonal  value so in a color scene you could have  two lines that are the same tonal value  but different colors  and your eye can tell them apart if you  shift to black and white and they have  the same  tonal value you won't be able to  distinguish between the two  because of this when you're shooting in  black and white or coloring in black and  white  you have to light or color the scenes  differently than you  would when using color in order to  maintain the same level of detail and  the same clarity in the scene  the upshot of this is that in the black  and white version of parasite there are  many scenes where  the darker elements of the scene have to  be brighter  so that we can see the details as  clearly as we can in the color version  as a result some of the scenes don't  feel as dark to me  in black and white as they do in color  this isn't really a big deal except in a  few crucial places  here the difference between the light  and dark areas of the scene  is actually important to the story and  selling the effect that the parks  can't see mr kim laying on the floor in  this shot in the black and white version  it doesn't feel like mr kim  is as much in darkness as he is in the  color version  not only is this area of the shot  actually  brighter in the black and white version  but in the color version we also get the  color contrast of the blue light from  the flashlight  mentally we tend to perceive blue light  as brighter than  warmer colors so the blueness of the  light here actually helps sell the  effect that mr kim is so in shadow that  the parks wouldn't notice him  i don't think it breaks the scene or the  film but it's a great example  of an effect that works really well in  color but isn't sold quite as well  in black and white i think parasite  suffers a little bit here because  it was shot in color if bong joon ho had  been working in black and white from the  start  he would have been able to shoot these  scenes in a way that would compensate  for any of the issues i'm talking about  here  black and white cinematography also has  the ability to alter the  emotion or feeling of certain scenes  it's hard to know exactly  how much of this effect is inherent to  black and white images  specifically and how much of this effect  just comes from the fact that we  tend to associate black and white images  with certain types of movies or a  certain period  in film history because as a viewer most  of the black and white movies you've  seen are probably a certain type of  older classic film with different  sensibilities  your brain is sort of subconsciously  attaching some of those sensibilities  and feelings that you're used to in  those kind of films  to anything else you see in black and  white the clearest example of this is  just how black and white films tend to  feel more  timeless and more classic simply because  most of the black and white films that  you've seen  are classics that have stood the test of  time watching parasite in black and  white  i think the film did feel more timeless  this effect  was undercut the most in scenes with  cell phones there's something about  seeing  a screen you almost always see in color  in black and white that creates a weird  sort of dissonance  overall i think the film felt less funny  to me in black and white but that could  come down just to the mood i was in this  time around watching the film  the most significant shift in the way a  scene felt to me  is the climactic action sequence  in black and white this felt more like a  battle or a conflict on some grander  scale maybe because it took on more of  the quality of like a western or  an older war movie previously this scene  had mostly seemed like  a chaotic scuffle with bursts of violent  action  rather than a war or a battle between  two forces  so watching the film in black and white  does add some new elements gives us a  different perspective on the film  but some things don't translate as well  and some things are lost  in the loss of color some compositions  and shots are more striking to me in  black and white than they are in color  but just as many don't work as well in  black and white as they did in color  for example the spectral highlights and  the blown out quality of the grass  in a scene like this just doesn't look  as good to me in black and white and it  seems kind of distracting whereas  in color i never really noticed it and  it didn't really bother me probably the  biggest thing that's lost in the  transition  is the emphasis of the greenery and  nature  that surrounds the rich family's house  the grass and the plants around the  house are so  vibrant saturated and green that really  stands in contrast  to the duller tones and the harsher  blues and oranges and yellows of the  kim's neighborhood and the area that  surrounds their house  the contrast of the pitiful little  smattering of  green weeds that are in front of the  kim's house against the  beautiful lush yard that's in front of  the park's house is  almost completely lost in the black and  white version the image of the rich  family on the grass during the party  kind of loses some of its pastoral  idealism that i think exists in the  color version  overall i think it's interesting to be  able to compare the two  and watch a film i've seen a bunch of  times in a different  format but i don't think the black and  white version benefits enough from the  changes to compensate for what's lost in  the transition  again a lot of this is probably due to  the fact that bong intended it as a  color film from the start so  if he had been shooting in black and  white from the start he would have  changed some things shot some things  differently to  work better with black and white if  you're a filmmaker even if you're not  interested in making a film in black and  white  studying what's different and the  benefits of black and white can help us  better understand the benefits and how  to use color filmmaking  i'm really glad bong jin ho decided to  make this version because seeing the  film from a different perspective  helps us learn more not just about the  film but about the differences  between color and black and white  filmmaking in general  if you got something out of this  breakdown please consider subscribing  and potentially becoming a patron and  finally i want to thank mubi for  sponsoring this video  if you want to dive deeper into the  world of black and white filmmaking  in mubi's library you can watch classic  black and white films like metropolis  nosferatu the general or battleship  potemkin or more recent black and white  efforts like  ida or the touring horse and many others  mubi is a hand curated online streaming  cinema where you can find  art house independent and foreign films  and that  space is almost exclusively where all  the black and white filmmaking takes  place these days i'm a big fan of mubi  it's my favorite place to discover  films that i wouldn't have even known  about otherwise you can try it now for  30 days and you'll have a chance to  check out their extensive library  or one of the new films that they add  every day  lubi is available all around the globe  for your extended 30-day free trial go  to  mubi.com thomasflight that's  movie.com thomasflight